What is BTC-Quantum-Shield?
BTC-Quantum-Shield is a research-grade framework for migrating and protecting Bitcoin wallets against quantum computer attacks using a custom Hybrid Name-Space (HNS) Protocol — a two-layer scheme that binds post-quantum identities to existing Bitcoin addresses.
Why Now?

A sufficiently powerful quantum computer running Shor's algorithm can break ECDSA (secp256k1) — the signature scheme Bitcoin relies on — exposing ~4 million BTC in P2PK outputs and all addresses with exposed public keys.

BTC-Quantum-Shield provides:

✅ Post-quantum key generation (CRYSTALS-Dilithium, FALCON, SPHINCS+)
✅ HNS address binding — linking quantum-safe identities to classical BTC addresses
✅ Safe migration paths for legacy wallets
✅ Taproot-compatible hybrid transaction signing
✅ Threshold signature support for multi-sig setups


🧠 The HNS Protocol — How It Works
The Hybrid Name-Space (HNS) Protocol is a two-phase commitment scheme:
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                      HNS PROTOCOL FLOW                           │
│                                                                  │
│  Phase 1 — ANCHOR                                                │
│  ┌─────────────────┐    hash(PQ_pubkey)    ┌──────────────────┐ │
│  │  Classic BTC    │ ──────────────────── ▶│  OP_RETURN TX    │ │
│  │  Address (ECDSA)│                       │  (on-chain anchor│ │
│  └─────────────────┘                       └──────────────────┘ │
│                                                                  │
│  Phase 2 — BIND                                                  │
│  ┌─────────────────┐    PQ_sig(BTC_addr)   ┌──────────────────┐ │
│  │  PQ Key Pair    │ ──────────────────── ▶│  HNS Registry    │ │
│  │  (Dilithium3)   │                       │  (IPFS / L2)     │ │
│  └─────────────────┘                       └──────────────────┘ │
│                                                                  │
│  Result: Ownership provable with PQ keys even after ECDSA break  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
Phase 1 — Anchor: You embed a hash of your new post-quantum public key inside a Bitcoin OP_RETURN output, committing to it on-chain before quantum threat materializes.
Phase 2 — Bind: You sign your classic Bitcoin address using your new PQ private key and register this in the HNS Registry (IPFS-based or L2 sidechain). This proves PQ ownership retroactively.
Migration: Once quantum risk is imminent, you broadcast a PQ-signed sweep transaction moving funds to a P2TR (Taproot) output secured by your PQ key.

